41 #edb/ref 17592186124248 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124444 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050997 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124983 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125189 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268897 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268898 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268899 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186268900 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335775 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338924 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338925 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338928 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383320 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406829 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124249 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124445 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125271 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125272 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125273 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125274 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125471 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1045 "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125271 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050164 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124747 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "27f87e6f-03c7-52b0-bd22-1d95ebba4299" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407346 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64972" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125189 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,-1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}(2xy-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ecca0679-247f-506b-95a3-b2be27b80569" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125189 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,-1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1) & =(1)^{2}(2(1)(-1)-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,-1) & ={f}_{y}(1,-1)(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3(y+1) \\\\ z-2 & =-3y-3 \\\\ z & =-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,-1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-3y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-3y-1$ (or $-3y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-3,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j-3\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125271 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125471 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125271 #edb/ref 1061 10304 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125271 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef70361b-fe3c-57f6-a037-19f320382e64" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125271 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125729 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125272 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125273 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125272 #edb/ref 1061 10305 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125272 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f75552c6-bdfd-5938-9f73-0458e5746656" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125272 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124443 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125273 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125274 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125273 #edb/ref 1061 10312 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125273 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2187e294-aaab-55c3-873b-46c11f1beb02" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125273 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124444 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1061 10315 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d77940fc-946a-53b1-a901-26cea615e37c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125274 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124445 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125471 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125272 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125471 #edb/ref 1061 23236 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125471 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50d32bc7-043e-58d2-a649-b9e1957fb923" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125471 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125814 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125729 #edb/ref 1064 "Just as the derivative of a function represents the slope of the tangent line to the function, the partial derivative of a function has a geometric interpretation as well.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the surface $z = f (x, y)= 18 - x^2 - y^2$ and the plane $x=3.$ The intersection of the surface and the plane forms a downward parabolic curve, as shown below. A point $P(3,2,5)$ is plotted and a tangent line is drawn through that point.\n\n![](images/86/8619c41cd0caf04ae9ee9bcae61e31739d8da583793a67c1c87e00186c3ac417.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent, we can compute the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ and evaluate it at $P(3,2,5)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(18-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2y \\\\ {\\frac{∂f}{∂y}|}_{P} & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To verify that this result is correct, we can find the equation of the downward parabolic curve and then use single-variable calculus to compute the slope of the tangent line.\n\nTo find the equation of the downward parabolic curve, we can substitute the equation of the plane ($x=3$) into the equation of the surface ($f (x, y)= 18 - x^2 - y^2$). Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,y) & =18-{3}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\  & =9-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the downward parabolic curve is $z=g(y) = 9-y^2.$ In the $yz$-plane, this curve has the following graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e982ef5d6d8d4fec9c64957d46f22cea2a85bd002595642f58700cf48cce4256.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent to $g(y)$ at $P,$ we can use single-variable calculus. We differentiate $g(y)$ and evaluate it at $P(3,2,5).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(y) & =-2y \\\\ {g}^{'}(2) & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125729 #edb/ref 1065 2376 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125729 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "56aef924-0db1-5d63-a9a3-5a81bcbdc411" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125729 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1064 "To recap, when we calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate $f$ with respect to $y$ and treat $x$ as a constant. This is the same as finding the intersection of $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $x = x_0$ and calculating the slope of the resulting curve.\n\nSo, we have the following result:\n\n*Suppose that a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a plane $x = x_0$ intersect on a curve $C,$ and suppose that $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ lies on $C.$ Then $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ evaluated at $P$ gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$*\n\nWe have a similar result for the intersection of a surface and a plane $y=y_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose that a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a plane $y = y_0$ intersect on a curve $C,$ and suppose that $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ lies on $C.$ Then $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ evaluated at $P$ gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1065 3933 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60c2d035-3f05-5f1d-b652-df42f673e869" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125814 #edb/ref 1067 "The Geometric Interpretation of the Partial Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266288 #edb/ref 1081 "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+9\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266288 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266288 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "106c62f5-6a15-5356-9819-bac29ddd8e00" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266830 #edb/ref 1081 "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266830 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266830 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "4cdcc8ed-799e-5868-8d9c-e5cddb211a2d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266862 #edb/ref 1081 "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+9\\end{aligned}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266862 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266862 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "502e00e2-2746-5fc9-8240-98511ddcf672" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592