f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05b78370-0b37-5800-aa28-6b6b17964b90" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407348 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407349 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407351 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407349 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8482a7f1-9b85-58c1-b946-6953c47668a6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407349 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407348 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407350 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407349 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407350 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1916473-66ad-5022-aafa-9787da440dac" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407350 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125189 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2c84ac0-3b38-584c-9809-dfa6181b9351" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186338923 #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
